! One Keyhive identity per PersonaGroup, 2026 ~ none above them

The operator ruled on 2026-10-18: "one Keyhive identity per PersonaGroup, none above them" (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road">> #/rulings). The operator gave the reason in their own words: "it's the only way to retain control over faces that don't know about each other". A vessel holds several faces; each face's PersonaGroup carries its own identity, event store, registry, archive and content-key seat. Faces that do not know each other keep apart on the wire, in removal, restore and custody.

A YIN and a YANG Luna-6 pair researched apart in two traded waves, then exchanged wave-two handbacks and checked one another's claims against code and house sources. The framing set seven stories across node and browser vessels: two faces and their private book, one-face device removal, a faceless herm, one-face restore, sibling admission, migration from vessel registration, and Keyhive 0.3.0-alpha.1's multi-instance boundary. The pair separated confirmed source ground from leads and left operator-owned forks visible.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/what-both-faces-hold>>

!! What both faces hold, sourced

* ''The vessel identity registers a union and delegates face bags vessel → veil → group.'' The vessel boots one Keyhive identity and registers every supplied bag as a union; face bags pass from vessel through veil to the face group. (`packages/lararium-keyhive/src/boot-daemon-keyhive.ts:95-105,185-188`; `packages/lararium-mesh/src/register-bags.ts:16-19,105-119`; `packages/lararium-keyhive/src/operator-daemon-behavior.ts:203-216`.)
* ''Each face's PersonaGroup gets born of its own veil.'' The veil Keyhive creates the face's group (`packages/lararium-keyhive/src/ceremony-core.ts:345-370`).
* ''Vessel and veils share one event store and one content-key holder.'' The same store initializes both providers (`ceremony-core.ts:346,361-365`); the mounted vessel process shares the holder across vessel and veil identities (`operator-daemon-behavior.ts:195-202,1036-1054`).
* ''Per-face archive rows already exist, but their contents do not yet guarantee face-exclusive state.'' Node carries vessel and per-face rows (`packages/lararium-node/src/identity-anchors.ts:124-168`); browser carries face rows (`browser-keyhive-archive.ts:18-31,69-93`). Those row names do not establish isolated event, archive or key contents.
* ''Keyhive 0.3.0-alpha.1 reads pre-alpha and unaudited.'' The repository pins that version (`packages/lararium-keyhive/package.json`); the vendored README calls the source pre-alpha, warns against production use, and says unaudited (`keyhive/README.md:8-18`).

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-lead>>

!! The lead ~ same-process instance isolation

Several Keyhive instances may hold object-local Rust maps, but that observation does not prove isolation from shared WASM state or globals (`keyhive/keyhive_core/src/keyhive.rs:85-132`; `keyhive-provider.ts:190-255`). Both faces mark same-process isolation a LEAD. They put the probe first: instantiate A and B with distinct signers, stores and archives; interleave registration, delegation and revocation; reboot/replay; attempt cross-reads and cross-mutation. Require clean separation throughout. If shared mutable state or callbacks cross, isolate each face hive in its own worker/process.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-build-order>>

!! K15 build order ~ red-first

# ''Probe the substrate, then make face contexts.'' Give A and B distinct signers, event stores and archives; interleave acts, reboot and cross-read. Red: neither resolves, exports, replays nor mutates the other's documents or events; then give each face its own store, registry, archive and key seat.
# ''Make the face the sole identity and group owner.'' Remove vessel delegation from face-bearing events. Red: capture two-face event bytes and fail on any shared creator or issuer; retain W25's creator-cleartext probe as regression.
# ''Route sessions and registries by one face.'' Give each session one face and each registry one owner; remove union registration. Red: A cannot list or resolve B's bag, and joining B cannot change A's registry.
# ''Seat content keys and removal per face.'' Keep each holder and causal roll within its face; build K12's fresh-group founder path here. Red: remove a work phone and verify work keys roll while Elyncia access stays; clock skew or a quiet replica never declares removal.
# ''Restore one face; constrain the herm.'' Restore only the selected face's group, events and keys; let a herm carry transport without face authorship. Red: restored A cannot list B; the herm cannot author or resolve any face document.
# ''Fence migration as one class change.'' Ship identity bytes, split stores and archive format together. Red: an old mixed archive refuses by name and requires re-found; no silent cross-face import.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/forks-and-house-readings>>

!! Four forks ~ house readings under operator confidence

* ''Place identity.'' Keep a floor-only transport key only behind a red test proving the herm cannot author, resolve or observe face capabilities; if it fails, keep no Keyhive identity.
* ''Runtime isolation.'' Permit one process only after the substrate probe proves separation; otherwise run each face hive in its own worker.
* ''Migration.'' Give co-mingled history fresh identities and name the refusal of an old mixed archive; build no bridge. Reopen only if evidence proves a history face-exclusive and a verified import can preserve that boundary.
* ''Founder removal.'' When the founder cannot leave by a revocable seat, found a fresh PersonaGroup. Reopen only if an authorized substrate path demonstrably removes the founder and rolls every reached bag; the present house reading stays fresh group.

<<~/ahu>>

