!! Testimony ~ Yang-Luna, wave two (verbatim)

```text
<<~ status "Yang-Luna: wave two ratchet complete; read-only source checks; Persona: Builder; yield follows" >>

# Yang-Luna: Lares (Tasked Spirit) · Persona: Builder

## Claims checked

Sources below are repository paths under `/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine`; inspected 2026-10-18. “CONFIRMED/REFUTED” means the cited source supports/contradicts the claim as phrased. Leads have not been established.

| Claim | From | Result | Source |
|---|---|---|---|
| One Keyhive identity per PersonaGroup, with none above faces, is the approved target. | Twin | CONFIRMED | `kh-framing.md` (operator’s 2026-10-18 framing); `stable-founding-road.mem` #/rulings. |
| Face veil creates the group but shares the vessel event store. | Twin | CONFIRMED | `packages/lararium-keyhive/src/ceremony-core.ts:345-370`; same `store` initializes both providers (`:346`, `:361-365`). |
| Vessel provider registers all supplied bags; face bags are delegated vessel→veil→group. | Twin | CONFIRMED | `boot-daemon-keyhive.ts:95-105,185-188`; `operator-daemon-behavior.ts:203-216`. |
| Registration currently unions all face memberships. | Twin | CONFIRMED | `packages/lararium-mesh/src/register-bags.ts:16-19,105-119`. This describes the current vessel model, not the approved one-face session target. |
| Clear creator identifiers can link face traffic when common vessel identity signs/creates it. | Twin | CONFIRMED | Ceremony explicitly says carried-event creator is clear and hence vessel Individual must not create group sentinels (`ceremony-core.ts:350-355`). Current delegation still passes through vessel identity (`operator-daemon-behavior.ts:203-216`); actual cross-face packet correlation remains to be tested. |
| Current archive carriers have vessel and per-face rows. | Twin | CONFIRMED | `identity-anchors.ts:124-168`; `browser-keyhive-archive.ts:18-31,69-93`. This is carrier layout, not evidence the rows leak on wire. |
| A content-key holder is beside vessel and veil identities and currently receives both providers. | Own | CONFIRMED | `operator-daemon-behavior.ts:195-202,1036-1054`. **Change of mind:** my wave-one wording that the holder was already face-seated overstated the implementation. Per-face seating is a target; current holder is shared by the mounted vessel process. |
| A single process can use multiple isolated Keyhive instances with separate event stores/archives. | Own | LEAD | Vendored `keyhive/keyhive_core/src/keyhive.rs:85-127` puts identity, groups, docs, pending events and ciphertext store on each `Keyhive` object. This supports object-local maps, but does not prove WASM/global-state or runtime isolation. Probe two instances with disjoint stores, interleaved operations, export/reboot, and cross-instance lookup; no conclusion from docs alone. |
| Herm may retain a floor/place identity with no face capability. | Twin / own | CONFIRMED | `boot-daemon-keyhive.ts:109-124` supports faceless boot; **target constraint** from framing: no identity above faces. Whether floor identity can be made incapable of face authorship needs a red test. |
| Removal is causal; group reach rolls documents, not sentinel itself. | Twin / own | CONFIRMED | `kapae.mem` #/keys; `sealed-store.mem` #/owed and #/the-content-key-holder; `stable-founding-road.mem` K12. Founder/implicit-seat removal and successor founding need explicit handling. |
| Current node/browser restore rows already provide one-face bundles. | Own | REFUTED | Carrier code stores face veil rows, but also vessel archive (`identity-anchors.ts:124-168`; browser rows `browser-keyhive-archive.ts:18-31`). That is not the approved selected-face-only bundle or fresh face identity restore. |
| Fresh per-face identity and archive split is the correct migration. | Twin / own | CONFIRMED | Ruling and fence in `stable-founding-road.mem` #/rulings; current shared store/registration paths above establish the migration need. Exact byte migration remains a design choice. |

## Converged story × vessel

| Story | Node path both faces can stand behind | Browser path both faces can stand behind |
|---|---|---|
| Two faces/private book | Each session receives exactly one face capability and that face’s Keyhive context; bag documents and content-key seat belong to that face. | Same per tab; separate face provider/store/archive and selected-face unlock. Wire events use face-scoped identity, never vessel ancestor. Current shared event store and vessel→veil delegation make this a required change, not present behavior. |
| Remove phone from work, keep Elyncia | Work’s causal group event drives only work bag rolls; Elyncia membership/archive/key state stays. Authorized remaining seat signs; phone departure alone does not impersonate revoker. | Same event semantics per tab/face. No clock-based removal. Creator-last case follows fresh-group/successor authority. |
| Faceless herm | Keep floor/place transport only if it cannot register, resolve, or author face documents; no Keyhive identity above faces. | N/A as browser face; an unconfigured browser has no face provider. |
| Restore one face | Bundle contains selected face group/archive/events and only its sealed key memory; fresh hearth rejoins that face. | Restore one selected face row and rejoin it in that tab; sibling rows remain unobserved. Existing rows alone do not establish this boundary. |
| Sibling joins | Join target PersonaGroup only; derive registration from its catalog and resolve no sibling URI. | Same target-face capability per tab; no union registry. |
| Migration | Replace vessel-wide provider registration with face contexts; split event stores and content-key seats; re-register per face. Treat identity bytes/archive shape as a pre-fence key-class change; reject ambiguous mixed history with a named re-found path. | Same model; selected-face archive rows and unlock. Re-found/reseal any co-mingled history; do not silently import it. |
| Multiple Keyhive instances | Object fields suggest viable per-instance ownership, but implementation waits on the probe below. | Same; WASM runtime isolation is not established by Rust object fields. |

## Build order and red tests

1. **Prove substrate instance boundary, then make face context/store/archive.** Red: instantiate A and B with distinct signers, event stores and archives; interleave register/delegate/revoke; assert neither resolves, exports, replays nor mutates the other’s docs/events after restart. If it fails, isolate instances in workers.
2. **Make face the sole identity/group owner.** Found and restore a group under its face identity; remove vessel creator/delegation from face-bearing events. Red: inspect captured event bytes/contact cards across two faces; fail on shared stable creator/issuer or cross-face correlation. W25 is a mandatory regression.
3. **Route sessions and registries by one capability.** Per-face catalog-derived bag set, no union; floor-only context cannot resolve face bags. Red: A cannot list/resolve B’s bag or ask B’s provider; joining B never changes A’s registry.
4. **Seat content keys and removal per face.** Lockbox archive and in-memory holder are scoped to one face; causal revocation rolls only reached bags. Red: remove work phone, verify work successor key excludes it while Elyncia read/version stays; skew and quiet replicas never declare removal; lock clears that face’s bare keys.
5. **Restore and herm boundary.** Red: selected-face bundle restores A and no B identity/events/keys; herm can transport but cannot author or resolve any face document.
6. **Fence migration.** Ship identity bytes, split stores, registration and archive format together before second-human data/habits. Red: old mixed archive refuses with explicit re-found/migration error; no silent cross-face import.

## Standing disagreements / probes

- **Keyhive runtime isolation:** own wave one leaned that multiple providers should work; Yin correctly kept same-process support conditional. I now keep the claim a LEAD. Probe from step 1 settles it; inspect module-level WASM state as well as interleaved operation and reboot behavior.
- **Wire linkability of present delegation:** source establishes the vessel→veil contact/delegation path and clear creator rule, but not the complete cross-face byte trace. Capture two-face join, shared-book delivery, and removal packets; compare creator, signer, event-store ordering, archive identifiers. Until then treat absence of a stable cross-face identifier as an acceptance test, not a proven fact.
- **Creator removal:** keep both readings: (a) re-found the group when its creator is the last/implicit seat; (b) successor founding if an authorized remaining quorum/path exists. Probe K12 against pinned Keyhive and road ruling with creator-alone and multi-seat cases; never model device departure as creator revocation.

## Operator-owned forks (current lean)

- **Place identity:** lean floor-only place identity if structural tests prove it cannot author or observe face caps; otherwise no Keyhive identity.
- **Face archive at rest:** lean separate existing custody rows/keystore per face with per-face unlock; operator still owns portability/recovery format. Flip if recovery requires a shared locator or principal.
- **Creator succession:** lean fresh face group when creator cannot causally leave; flip only if Keyhive/ruling probe proves an authorized successor transition without a shared ancestor.
- **Migration:** lean fresh identity for any co-mingled event history and hard refusal before the fence; flip only for history proven face-exclusive and a verified byte-preserving import.
- **Same-process instances:** lean only after substrate probe; use worker isolation if it reveals shared mutable runtime state.

